// Class 12 Physics practice bank, part 1: chapters 1-7 (Electrostatics to Alternating Current).
StudyData.addPractice(12, "physics", {
  "electric-charges-and-fields": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The SI unit of electric flux is:", options: ["N m² C⁻²", "N m² C⁻¹", "N C⁻¹", "C m⁻²"], answer: 1, explain: "Flux Φ = E·A, so its unit is (N C⁻¹)(m²) = N m² C⁻¹ (equivalently V m).", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which of the following charges cannot exist on a body?", options: ["3.2 × 10⁻¹⁹ C", "4.8 × 10⁻¹⁹ C", "2.4 × 10⁻¹⁹ C", "8.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ C"], answer: 2, explain: "Charge is quantised, q = ne. 2.4 × 10⁻¹⁹ C is 1.5e, not a whole-number multiple of e = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Electric field lines never intersect because:", options: ["they carry equal and opposite charges", "the field is always zero at a crossing", "they would form closed loops", "two tangents at the point would give two directions of the field there"], answer: 3, explain: "The field at a point has one definite direction; at a crossing there would be two tangents, which is impossible.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The SI unit of electric dipole moment is:", options: ["C m", "C m⁻¹", "N m", "C² m"], answer: 0, explain: "p = q × 2a, so its unit is coulomb-metre (C m).", level: 1 },
      { q: "The net electric flux through a closed surface enclosing an electric dipole (charges +q and −q) is:", options: ["q/ε₀", "2q/ε₀", "zero", "infinite"], answer: 2, explain: "By Gauss's law, flux = (net enclosed charge)/ε₀ = (q − q)/ε₀ = 0.", level: 1 },

      { q: "Two point charges 2 μC and 3 μC are placed 3 cm apart in air. The force between them is (k = 9 × 10⁹ N m² C⁻²):", options: ["60 N", "6 N", "180 N", "20 N"], answer: 0, explain: "F = kq₁q₂/r² = 9 × 10⁹ × 2 × 10⁻⁶ × 3 × 10⁻⁶ / (0.03)² = 0.054 / 9 × 10⁻⁴ = 60 N (repulsive).", level: 2 },
      { q: "The electric field at a distance of 30 cm from a point charge of 3 nC in air is:", options: ["90 N/C", "300 N/C", "900 N/C", "100 N/C"], answer: 1, explain: "E = kq/r² = 9 × 10⁹ × 3 × 10⁻⁹ / (0.3)² = 27 / 0.09 = 300 N/C.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): The net force on an electric dipole placed in a uniform electric field is zero.\nReason (R): A dipole in a uniform field experiences a torque unless it is aligned along the field.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 1, explain: "The two forces +qE and −qE cancel, so the net force is zero. R is also true (τ = pE sinθ) but it speaks of torque, not force, so it does not explain A.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "While combing dry hair, a student's plastic comb gains a charge of −4.8 nC. How many electrons were transferred to the comb?", options: ["3 × 10⁹", "3 × 10¹¹", "7.7 × 10⁹", "3 × 10¹⁰"], answer: 3, explain: "n = q/e = 4.8 × 10⁻⁹ / 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 3 × 10¹⁰ electrons.", level: 2 },
      { q: "For a short dipole, the ratio of the electric field at a point on the axis to that at an equal distance on the equatorial line is:", options: ["1/2", "1", "2", "4"], answer: 2, explain: "E(axial) = 2kp/r³ and E(equatorial) = kp/r³, so the ratio is 2.", level: 2 },

      { q: "Two large parallel sheets carry uniform surface charge densities +σ and −2σ. The field between the sheets and outside them is respectively:", options: ["σ/ε₀ between, zero outside", "σ/2ε₀ between, 3σ/2ε₀ outside", "3σ/2ε₀ between, σ/2ε₀ outside", "σ/2ε₀ in each region"], answer: 2, explain: "Each sheet gives σ/2ε₀ per unit σ. Between the sheets the fields add: σ/2ε₀ + 2σ/2ε₀ = 3σ/2ε₀. Outside they oppose: 2σ/2ε₀ − σ/2ε₀ = σ/2ε₀.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A point charge q is placed at one corner of a cube. The electric flux through one of the faces that does NOT touch that corner is:", options: ["q/8ε₀", "q/48ε₀", "q/6ε₀", "q/24ε₀"], answer: 3, explain: "Eight such cubes around the corner enclose q, so the flux through the given cube is q/8ε₀. The three faces meeting at the corner carry zero flux; the other three share it equally: q/24ε₀ each.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Case: A student has two identical metal spheres. Sphere A carries +6 μC and sphere B carries −2 μC. She touches them together and then places them back at their original separation.\nThe force between them is now:", options: ["1/3 of the original, attractive", "1/3 of the original, repulsive", "3 times the original, repulsive", "1/2 of the original, repulsive"], answer: 1, explain: "After contact each sphere has (6 − 2)/2 = +2 μC. Original |q₁q₂| = 12, new q₁q₂ = 4, so the force is 1/3 as large and now repulsive (both positive).", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Gauss's law is of practical use for finding the electric field only when the charge distribution has high symmetry.\nReason (R): Gauss's law is valid only for symmetric charge distributions.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 2, explain: "Gauss's law holds for every closed surface and any charge distribution (R is false). It is only easy to apply when symmetry lets E be taken out of the integral (A is true).", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Define electric dipole moment. Write its SI unit and direction.", a: "Electric dipole moment p = q × 2a, the product of either charge and the separation between the charges. SI unit: coulomb metre (C m). It is a vector directed from the negative charge to the positive charge.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "What is the net electric flux through a closed surface that encloses no net charge? Give a reason.", a: "Zero. By Gauss's law, flux = q(enclosed)/ε₀, and the enclosed net charge is zero.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Calculate the force between a 5 μC and a −3 μC charge placed 15 cm apart in air. State whether it is attractive or repulsive.", a: "F = k|q₁q₂|/r² = 9 × 10⁹ × 5 × 10⁻⁶ × 3 × 10⁻⁶ / (0.15)²\n= 0.135 / 0.0225 = **6 N**.\nThe charges are unlike, so the force is attractive.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "State two properties of electric field lines. Why can they not form closed loops for a static charge distribution?", a: "Properties: (1) they start from positive charges and end on negative charges, and never cross one another; (2) the tangent at a point gives the direction of E, and closer lines mean a stronger field.\nElectrostatic field is conservative (the work done around a closed path is zero), but a closed field line would make ∮E·dl ≠ 0, so closed loops are not possible.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Charges of +4 μC and +1 μC are placed 30 cm apart in air. Find the point on the line joining them where the net electric field is zero.", a: "Between two like charges the field cancels at a point x from the 4 μC charge.\nSet k(4 μC)/x² = k(1 μC)/(0.3 − x)².\nTaking roots: 2/x = 1/(0.3 − x) → 2(0.3 − x) = x → 0.6 = 3x → x = 0.2 m.\nThe neutral point is **20 cm from the 4 μC charge** (10 cm from the 1 μC charge). Check: 4/0.04 = 100 = 1/0.01.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Three charges of +2 μC each are placed at the corners of an equilateral triangle of side 10 cm. Find the magnitude of the net force on one of them.", a: "Force between any two: F = 9 × 10⁹ × (2 × 10⁻⁶)² / (0.1)² = 0.036 / 0.01 = 3.6 N.\nThe two forces on one charge are each 3.6 N, at 60° to each other.\nResultant = 2F cos30° = 2 × 3.6 × (√3/2) = 3.6√3 ≈ **6.2 N**, directed along the bisector away from the other two charges.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Derive the expression for the torque on an electric dipole placed in a uniform electric field.", a: "1. Dipole of charges ±q, separation 2a, makes angle θ with a uniform field E.\n2. Forces: +qE along E and −qE opposite. Net force = 0, but they form a couple.\n3. Perpendicular distance between the lines of action = 2a sinθ.\n4. Torque τ = qE × 2a sinθ = (q·2a)E sinθ = **pE sinθ**, i.e. τ = p × E (vector form), tending to align p with E.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Using Gauss's law, derive the electric field due to an infinitely long, thin, straight wire of uniform linear charge density λ.", a: "1. By symmetry, E is radial and has the same magnitude at all points at distance r from the wire.\n2. Take a coaxial cylindrical Gaussian surface of radius r and length l.\n3. Flux through the two end caps = 0 (E is parallel to their surface). Through the curved surface, flux = E × 2πrl.\n4. Charge enclosed = λl. Gauss's law: E(2πrl) = λl/ε₀.\n5. **E = λ/(2πε₀r)**, directed radially outward for λ > 0 (inward for λ < 0). E ∝ 1/r.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Derive the expression for the electric field of a short electric dipole (a) at a point on its axis and (b) at a point on its equatorial line.", a: "Dipole ±q, separation 2a, centre O; p = 2aq.\n(a) Axial point P at distance r from O (on the side of +q):\n1. E₊ = kq/(r − a)² along p; E₋ = kq/(r + a)² opposite to p.\n2. E = kq[1/(r − a)² − 1/(r + a)²] = kq · 4ar /(r² − a²)².\n3. For r ≫ a: **E = 2kp/r³** (along p), i.e. p/(2πε₀r³).\n(b) Equatorial point at distance r:\n1. Each charge is at distance √(r² + a²); |E₊| = |E₋| = kq/(r² + a²).\n2. Components perpendicular to the axis cancel; components parallel to the axis add (opposite to p): E = 2 · kq/(r² + a²) · cosθ with cosθ = a/√(r² + a²).\n3. E = kp/(r² + a²)^(3/2); for r ≫ a, **E = kp/r³**, directed opposite to p.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: A thin spherical shell of radius 10 cm carries a total charge of +8 nC spread uniformly over its surface. A student uses Gauss's law to find the field at different distances from its centre.\n(i) Find the electric field at a point 20 cm from the centre.\n(ii) What is the field at a point 5 cm from the centre?\n(iii) Find the field just at the surface of the shell.", a: "(i) Outside, the shell acts like a point charge at its centre: E = kQ/r² = 9 × 10⁹ × 8 × 10⁻⁹ / (0.2)² = 72 / 0.04 = **1800 N/C**, radially outward.\n(ii) Inside the shell no charge is enclosed by a Gaussian sphere, so **E = 0**.\n(iii) At r = 0.1 m: E = 72 / (0.1)² = **7200 N/C**, radially outward.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "electrostatic-potential-and-capacitance": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "One farad is equal to:", options: ["1 C V", "1 J C⁻¹", "1 C V⁻¹", "1 V C⁻¹"], answer: 2, explain: "C = Q/V, so 1 F = 1 coulomb per volt.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The electric potential due to a point charge varies with the distance r from it as:", options: ["1/r²", "r", "r²", "1/r"], answer: 3, explain: "V = kq/r, so V ∝ 1/r (the field varies as 1/r²).", level: 1 },
      { q: "The work done in moving a test charge between two points on the same equipotential surface is:", options: ["zero", "equal to qE", "maximum", "equal to qV"], answer: 0, explain: "W = q(V_B − V_A) = 0 since the potential is the same at both points.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The relation between electric field and potential in one dimension is:", options: ["E = V/r", "E = −dV/dr", "E = V·r", "E = dV/dr"], answer: 1, explain: "The field is the negative gradient of the potential: E = −dV/dr; it points from higher to lower potential.", level: 1 },
      { q: "If the space between the plates of a capacitor is completely filled with a dielectric of constant K, the capacitance becomes:", options: ["C₀/K", "C₀ + K", "KC₀", "K²C₀"], answer: 2, explain: "C = KC₀, since the dielectric reduces the field (and hence the potential difference) for the same charge.", level: 1 },

      { q: "The electric potential at a distance of 0.9 m from a point charge of 10 nC in air is:", options: ["10 V", "100 V", "1000 V", "900 V"], answer: 1, explain: "V = kq/r = 9 × 10⁹ × 10 × 10⁻⁹ / 0.9 = 90 / 0.9 = 100 V.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Capacitors of 2 μF and 3 μF are connected in series. The equivalent capacitance is:", options: ["5 μF", "0.83 μF", "6 μF", "1.2 μF"], answer: 3, explain: "C = (2 × 3)/(2 + 3) = 6/5 = 1.2 μF.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): The electric field can be non-zero at a point where the potential is zero.\nReason (R): The electric field is the negative gradient of the potential, so it depends on how V changes in space, not on the value of V itself.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "On the equatorial line of a dipole V = 0 but E ≠ 0. Since E = −dV/dr, E depends on the slope of V, which is why R explains A.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "A camera flash unit uses a 100 μF capacitor charged to 300 V. The energy stored, released as a flash, is:", options: ["0.45 J", "1.5 J", "4.5 J", "9 J"], answer: 2, explain: "U = ½CV² = ½ × 100 × 10⁻⁶ × (300)² = ½ × 10⁻⁴ × 9 × 10⁴ = 4.5 J.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A parallel plate capacitor has plate area 100 cm² and plate separation 1 mm in air (ε₀ = 8.85 × 10⁻¹² F m⁻¹). Its capacitance is about:", options: ["88.5 pF", "8.85 pF", "885 pF", "0.885 pF"], answer: 0, explain: "C = ε₀A/d = 8.85 × 10⁻¹² × 10⁻² / 10⁻³ = 8.85 × 10⁻¹¹ F = 88.5 pF.", level: 2 },

      { q: "A slab of dielectric constant 3 and thickness 3 mm is placed between the plates of an air capacitor with separation 6 mm. The capacitance becomes:", options: ["1.2 times", "1.5 times", "2 times", "3 times"], answer: 1, explain: "Effective separation = d − t + t/K = 6 − 3 + 1 = 4 mm. C/C₀ = 6/4 = 1.5.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A 4 μF capacitor charged to 100 V is connected to an uncharged 6 μF capacitor. The loss of energy in the process is:", options: ["8 mJ", "20 mJ", "4 mJ", "12 mJ"], answer: 3, explain: "Common potential V = 4 × 100/(4 + 6) = 40 V. U_i = ½ × 4 × 10⁻⁶ × 100² = 20 mJ; U_f = ½ × 10 × 10⁻⁶ × 40² = 8 mJ. Loss = 12 mJ (lost as heat and radiation).", level: 3 },
      { q: "Case: In a laboratory, three small spheres each carrying +1 μC are brought from far away and fixed at the corners of an equilateral triangle of side 0.3 m.\nThe total work done by the external agent (the electrostatic potential energy of the system) is:", options: ["0.03 J", "0.27 J", "0.09 J", "0.18 J"], answer: 2, explain: "There are 3 pairs: U = 3 × kq²/a = 3 × 9 × 10⁹ × 10⁻¹² / 0.3 = 0.09 J.", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Two equipotential surfaces can intersect each other.\nReason (R): The electric field is perpendicular to an equipotential surface at every point.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 3, explain: "R is true. If two such surfaces intersected, the field at the common point would need to be perpendicular to both, i.e. have two directions, which is impossible. So A is false.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Define the dielectric constant of a medium.", a: "The dielectric constant K is the ratio of the capacitance with the dielectric between the plates to the capacitance with vacuum: K = C/C₀ = ε/ε₀ (it is dimensionless).", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Why is the electric potential the same at all points inside and on the surface of a charged conductor?", a: "In electrostatics the field inside a conductor is zero, so no work is done in moving a charge between any two points of it. Hence ΔV = 0 and the whole conductor is an equipotential.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Find the work done by the electric field in moving a charge of 2 μC from a point at potential 50 V to a point at 20 V.", a: "W = q(V_A − V_B) = 2 × 10⁻⁶ × (50 − 20)\n= 6 × 10⁻⁵ J = **60 μJ** (positive, as the charge moves to a lower potential).", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Describe the shape of equipotential surfaces for (i) an isolated point charge and (ii) a uniform electric field.", a: "(i) Concentric spheres centred on the charge, since V = kq/r is constant at fixed r; the field lines are radial and perpendicular to them.\n(ii) Planes perpendicular to the field direction, equally spaced for equal potential differences.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "A 9 μF capacitor is connected in series with a parallel combination of 3 μF and 6 μF capacitors, and a 90 V supply is applied across the whole network. Find the equivalent capacitance and the charge on each capacitor.", a: "Parallel part: 3 + 6 = 9 μF.\nSeries with 9 μF: C = (9 × 9)/(9 + 9) = **4.5 μF**.\nTotal charge Q = CV = 4.5 μF × 90 V = **405 μC**; this is the charge on the 9 μF capacitor.\nVoltage across the parallel part = 405/9 = 45 V, so charges are 3 × 45 = **135 μC** on 3 μF and 6 × 45 = **270 μC** on 6 μF (sum 405 μC).", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Derive the formula for the equivalent capacitance of three capacitors C₁, C₂, C₃ connected in series.", a: "1. In series, the same charge Q is on each capacitor.\n2. Potential differences: V₁ = Q/C₁, V₂ = Q/C₂, V₃ = Q/C₃.\n3. Total V = V₁ + V₂ + V₃ = Q(1/C₁ + 1/C₂ + 1/C₃).\n4. Equivalent C = Q/V gives **1/C = 1/C₁ + 1/C₂ + 1/C₃**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "A 20 pF parallel plate capacitor is charged to 100 V and the battery is then disconnected. A dielectric slab of K = 5 is inserted to fill the gap. Find the energy stored before and after, and the new potential difference.", a: "Initial energy U₁ = ½CV² = ½ × 20 × 10⁻¹² × 100² = 10⁻⁷ J = **100 nJ**.\nCharge stays constant: Q = 20 pF × 100 V = 2 nC. New C = 5 × 20 = 100 pF.\nNew V = Q/C = 2 × 10⁻⁹ / 10⁻¹⁰ = **20 V**.\nFinal energy U₂ = Q²/2C = (2 × 10⁻⁹)² / (2 × 10⁻¹⁰) = 2 × 10⁻⁸ J = **20 nJ** (energy drops to 1/K).", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Derive an expression for the capacitance of a parallel plate capacitor (a) with air between the plates and (b) when a dielectric slab of thickness t < d and constant K is inserted.", a: "(a) 1. Plates of area A with charges ±Q; surface density σ = Q/A.\n2. Field between plates (ignoring edge effects): E = σ/ε₀ = Q/(ε₀A).\n3. Potential difference V = Ed = Qd/(ε₀A).\n4. **C = Q/V = ε₀A/d**.\n(b) 1. Field in air gap E₀ = σ/ε₀; field in the slab E = E₀/K.\n2. V = E₀(d − t) + E₀t/K = (σ/ε₀)[d − t + t/K].\n3. **C = ε₀A / (d − t + t/K)**.\n4. For t = d this gives C = Kε₀A/d = KC₀, and C > C₀ in all cases.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Derive an expression for the electric potential at a point P (distance r from the centre, at angle θ to the dipole axis) due to a short electric dipole. Hence find the potential on the axial and equatorial lines.", a: "1. Dipole ±q at B and A, separation 2a, centre O, p = 2aq along OB.\n2. Let r₁ = distance of P from +q and r₂ from −q. V = kq(1/r₁ − 1/r₂).\n3. For r ≫ a: r₁ ≈ r − a cosθ and r₂ ≈ r + a cosθ.\n4. V = kq [ (r₂ − r₁)/(r₁r₂) ] = kq (2a cosθ)/(r² − a²cos²θ) ≈ kq·2a cosθ/r².\n5. **V = kp cosθ / r²** = p cosθ/(4πε₀r²).\nAxial (θ = 0° or 180°): V = ±kp/r². Equatorial (θ = 90°): **V = 0**. The potential falls as 1/r² (faster than 1/r for a single charge).", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: A parallel plate capacitor has plates of area 200 cm² separated by 2 mm of air, and is connected to a 100 V battery. The battery is then disconnected and the gap is completely filled with mica of dielectric constant 6. (ε₀ = 8.85 × 10⁻¹² F m⁻¹)\n(i) Find the capacitance with air between the plates.\n(ii) Find the charge on the plates.\n(iii) Find the potential difference after the mica is inserted.", a: "(i) C = ε₀A/d = 8.85 × 10⁻¹² × 200 × 10⁻⁴ / (2 × 10⁻³) = 8.85 × 10⁻¹¹ F ≈ **88.5 pF**.\n(ii) Q = CV = 8.85 × 10⁻¹¹ × 100 = 8.85 × 10⁻⁹ C ≈ **8.85 nC**.\n(iii) The battery is disconnected, so Q is constant while C becomes 6 times larger: V′ = V/K = 100/6 ≈ **16.7 V**.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "current-electricity": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The SI unit of resistivity is:", options: ["Ω", "Ω m⁻¹", "Ω m", "Ω m²"], answer: 2, explain: "ρ = RA/l, so its unit is ohm × m²/m = ohm metre (Ω m).", level: 1 },
      { q: "A conductor of cross-section A has n free electrons per unit volume with drift velocity v_d. The current is:", options: ["I = nAev_d", "I = nev_d/A", "I = nAe/v_d", "I = Aev_d/n"], answer: 0, explain: "In time t, charge nAe·v_d·t crosses a section, so I = nAev_d.", level: 1 },
      { q: "When the temperature of a metallic conductor rises, its resistance:", options: ["first decreases then increases", "decreases", "remains constant", "increases"], answer: 3, explain: "More frequent collisions (smaller relaxation time τ) increase the resistivity of a metal.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Kirchhoff's junction rule is a consequence of conservation of:", options: ["energy", "charge", "momentum", "mass"], answer: 1, explain: "Charge cannot accumulate at a junction, so current in = current out. (The loop rule expresses energy conservation.)", level: 1 },
      { q: "A Wheatstone bridge with arms P, Q, R, S is balanced when:", options: ["P/Q = R/S", "P/Q = S/R", "PQ = RS", "P + Q = R + S"], answer: 0, explain: "No current flows through the galvanometer when P/Q = R/S.", level: 1 },

      { q: "A cell of emf 12 V and internal resistance 1 Ω is connected to an external resistance of 5 Ω. The potential difference across the terminals of the cell is:", options: ["12 V", "10 V", "6 V", "2 V"], answer: 1, explain: "I = E/(R + r) = 12/6 = 2 A. Terminal voltage V = IR = 2 × 5 = 10 V (= E − Ir).", level: 2 },
      { q: "A wire of resistance R is uniformly stretched to double its length (volume constant). The new resistance is:", options: ["2R", "R/2", "4R", "8R"], answer: 2, explain: "R = ρl²/V, so doubling l gives 4 times the resistance.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): The drift velocity of electrons in a wire is of the order of 10⁻⁴ m s⁻¹, yet a bulb lights almost instantly when the switch is closed.\nReason (R): The electric field is set up along the whole wire at nearly the speed of light and makes all the free electrons drift together.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "Free electrons are present throughout the wire, including in the filament. The field establishes in the whole circuit almost at once, so they all start drifting immediately.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "A student measures a dry cell: a voltmeter reads 1.5 V across it in open circuit. When a 2.8 Ω resistor is connected across it, the voltmeter reads 1.4 V. The internal resistance of the cell is:", options: ["0.1 Ω", "0.5 Ω", "2.0 Ω", "0.2 Ω"], answer: 3, explain: "I = V/R = 1.4/2.8 = 0.5 A. Lost voltage = 1.5 − 1.4 = 0.1 V = Ir, so r = 0.1/0.5 = 0.2 Ω.", level: 2 },
      { q: "In a balanced Wheatstone bridge, P = 10 Ω, Q = 20 Ω and R = 15 Ω, with P/Q = R/S. The value of S is:", options: ["7.5 Ω", "45 Ω", "30 Ω", "15 Ω"], answer: 2, explain: "P/Q = R/S gives S = RQ/P = 15 × 20/10 = 30 Ω.", level: 2 },

      { q: "Two cells (E₁ = 4 V, r₁ = 1 Ω) and (E₂ = 2 V, r₂ = 1 Ω) are connected in parallel with their like terminals joined, across an external resistor R = 1 Ω. The current through R is:", options: ["2 A", "3 A", "1 A", "6 A"], answer: 0, explain: "Let V be the voltage across R. Junction rule: (4 − V)/1 + (2 − V)/1 = V/1 → 6 = 3V, V = 2 V. So I = 2 A (the second cell supplies no current).", level: 3 },
      { q: "A potentiometer wire of length 1 m and resistance 10 Ω is connected in series with a 5 Ω resistor and a 3 V cell of negligible internal resistance. The potential gradient along the wire is:", options: ["0.2 V/m", "3 V/m", "0.6 V/m", "2 V/m"], answer: 3, explain: "I = 3/(10 + 5) = 0.2 A. Voltage across the wire = 0.2 × 10 = 2 V over 1 m, so the gradient is 2 V m⁻¹.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Case: In a heating experiment, the resistance of a metal coil is 10 Ω at 20 °C. The temperature coefficient of resistance of its material is 4 × 10⁻³ °C⁻¹ (treat as constant).\nThe resistance of the coil at 70 °C is:", options: ["11 Ω", "14 Ω", "12 Ω", "13.5 Ω"], answer: 2, explain: "R = R₀[1 + α(T − T₀)] = 10[1 + 4 × 10⁻³ × 50] = 10 × 1.2 = 12 Ω.", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): When the terminals of a cell are joined directly by a thick wire of negligible resistance, the current is E/r.\nReason (R): Under this condition the terminal voltage of the cell equals its emf.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 2, explain: "With R ≈ 0, I = E/(R + r) = E/r, so A is true. The terminal voltage V = E − Ir = 0, not E, so R is false.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Define drift velocity of free electrons in a conductor.", a: "Drift velocity is the average velocity with which free electrons move (opposite to the electric field) in a conductor under an applied electric field, superposed on their random thermal motion: v_d = −(eEτ)/m.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "State Kirchhoff's loop rule.", a: "The algebraic sum of the changes in potential around any closed loop of a circuit is zero (ΣIR + Σε = 0 with proper signs). It follows from conservation of energy.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "A copper wire of cross-section 1 mm² carries a current of 2 A. If the number density of free electrons in copper is 8.5 × 10²⁸ m⁻³, find the drift velocity.", a: "I = nAev_d → v_d = I/(nAe)\n= 2 / (8.5 × 10²⁸ × 10⁻⁶ × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹)\n= 2 / (1.36 × 10⁴)\n≈ **1.5 × 10⁻⁴ m s⁻¹**.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "A cell of emf 2 V and internal resistance 0.5 Ω is connected across a resistor of 4.5 Ω. Find the current in the circuit and the terminal voltage of the cell.", a: "I = E/(R + r) = 2/(4.5 + 0.5) = **0.4 A**.\nTerminal voltage V = E − Ir = 2 − 0.4 × 0.5 = **1.8 V** (also IR = 0.4 × 4.5 = 1.8 V).", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Two cells of emfs E₁, E₂ and internal resistances r₁, r₂ are connected in parallel (like terminals joined). Derive the equivalent emf and internal resistance.", a: "1. Let the common terminal voltage be V. Currents: I₁ = (E₁ − V)/r₁, I₂ = (E₂ − V)/r₂.\n2. Total I = I₁ + I₂ = (E₁/r₁ + E₂/r₂) − V(1/r₁ + 1/r₂).\n3. Write V = E_eq − I r_eq: V = (E₁r₂ + E₂r₁)/(r₁ + r₂) − I · r₁r₂/(r₁ + r₂).\n4. Hence **E_eq = (E₁r₂ + E₂r₁)/(r₁ + r₂)** and **r_eq = r₁r₂/(r₁ + r₂)** (i.e. 1/r_eq = 1/r₁ + 1/r₂).", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Using Kirchhoff's rules, find the currents in the network: an 8 V cell with 1 Ω internal resistance, and a 7 V cell with 1 Ω internal resistance, are joined with a common 2 Ω resistor so that both drive current through it in the same direction.", a: "Let I₁ flow from the 8 V cell and I₂ from the 7 V cell; the 2 Ω resistor carries I₁ + I₂.\nLoop 1: 8 − 1·I₁ − 2(I₁ + I₂) = 0 → 3I₁ + 2I₂ = 8 … (1)\nLoop 2: 7 − 1·I₂ − 2(I₁ + I₂) = 0 → 2I₁ + 3I₂ = 7 … (2)\n3 × (1) − 2 × (2): 5I₁ = 10, so **I₁ = 2 A**; then I₂ = **1 A**.\nCurrent through the 2 Ω resistor = I₁ + I₂ = **3 A**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "In a metre bridge, the unknown resistance X is in the left gap and a 12 Ω resistor in the right gap. The null point is at 40 cm from the left end. Find X. Also find the new null-point length if the 12 Ω resistor is replaced by 18 Ω.", a: "At balance X/12 = l/(100 − l) = 40/60 → **X = 8 Ω**.\nWith 18 Ω in the right gap: 8/18 = l/(100 − l) → 8(100 − l) = 18l → 800 = 26l → l ≈ **30.8 cm** from the left end.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Using the Drude model, derive the relation ρ = m/(ne²τ) for a conductor and show that it leads to Ohm's law.", a: "1. A free electron in field E has acceleration a = −eE/m.\n2. Between collisions (average time τ, relaxation time) it gains velocity; the average drift velocity is v_d = aτ = −eEτ/m.\n3. Current I = neAv_d in magnitude, so current density J = I/A = ne|v_d| = ne²τE/m.\n4. Compare with J = σE: conductivity σ = ne²τ/m, so **resistivity ρ = 1/σ = m/(ne²τ)**.\n5. With E = V/l and J = I/A: I/A = σV/l → V = I(ρl/A) = IR, with R = ρl/A constant at constant temperature: this is Ohm's law. ρ depends on n and τ, not on the size of the conductor.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "State the principle of a Wheatstone bridge and use Kirchhoff's rules to derive its balance condition.", a: "Four resistors P, Q, R, S form a quadrilateral ABCD (P = AB, Q = BC, R = AD, S = DC) with a cell between A and C and a galvanometer between B and D.\n1. Let currents be: I₁ through P, I₂ through Q (in the balanced state), I₃ through R, I₄ through S. The galvanometer current is I_g.\n2. Balance means I_g = 0, so by the junction rule at B and D: I₁ = I₂ and I₃ = I₄.\n3. Loop ABDA (galvanometer resistance G, I_g = 0): −I₁P + I₃R = 0, so I₁P = I₃R … (a).\n4. Loop BCDB: −I₂Q + I₄S = 0, so I₁Q = I₃S … (b).\n5. Divide (a) by (b): **P/Q = R/S**. The bridge is balanced and the galvanometer reads zero. Principle: used to find an unknown resistance when the other three are known.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: A potentiometer wire of length 10 m and resistance 20 Ω is connected in series with a 20 Ω resistor and a 4 V driver cell of negligible internal resistance. A cell A balances at 7.5 m of the wire. When a 5 Ω resistor is connected across cell A, the balance length shifts to 6.0 m.\n(i) Find the potential gradient of the wire.\n(ii) Find the emf of cell A.\n(iii) Find the internal resistance of cell A.", a: "(i) I = 4/(20 + 20) = 0.1 A. Voltage across wire = 0.1 × 20 = 2 V over 10 m, so the gradient is **0.2 V m⁻¹**.\n(ii) E = 0.2 × 7.5 = **1.5 V**.\n(iii) Terminal voltage with 5 Ω across the cell: V = 0.2 × 6.0 = 1.2 V.\nr = R(E − V)/V = 5 × (1.5 − 1.2)/1.2 = **1.25 Ω**.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "moving-charges-and-magnetism": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The SI unit of magnetic field is:", options: ["weber", "henry", "tesla", "ampere"], answer: 2, explain: "The tesla (T) = N A⁻¹ m⁻¹ = Wb m⁻². The weber is the unit of flux and the henry of inductance.", level: 1 },
      { q: "A charged particle moves parallel to a uniform magnetic field. The magnetic force on it is:", options: ["zero", "maximum", "qvB/2", "qvB cos θ"], answer: 0, explain: "F = qvB sinθ and θ = 0° for motion along the field, so F = 0.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The magnetic field well inside a long solenoid carrying current I with n turns per unit length is:", options: ["μ₀I/2πn", "zero", "μ₀I/n", "μ₀nI"], answer: 3, explain: "Ampère's law gives B = μ₀nI, uniform and along the axis.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The direction of the magnetic field around a long straight current-carrying wire is given by:", options: ["Fleming's left-hand rule", "the right-hand thumb rule", "Lenz's law", "Fleming's right-hand rule"], answer: 1, explain: "Point the right thumb along the current; the curled fingers show the circular field lines.", level: 1 },
      { q: "A moving coil galvanometer is converted into a voltmeter by connecting:", options: ["a low resistance in parallel", "a low resistance in series", "a high resistance in parallel", "a high resistance in series"], answer: 3, explain: "A large series resistance limits the current through the galvanometer and makes the voltmeter resistance high.", level: 1 },

      { q: "A circular loop of radius 5 cm carries a current of 2 A. The magnetic field at its centre is nearly (μ₀ = 4π × 10⁻⁷ T m A⁻¹):", options: ["2.5 × 10⁻⁶ T", "1.26 × 10⁻⁵ T", "2.5 × 10⁻⁵ T", "5.0 × 10⁻⁵ T"], answer: 2, explain: "B = μ₀I/2R = 4π × 10⁻⁷ × 2 / (2 × 0.05) = 8π × 10⁻⁶ ≈ 2.5 × 10⁻⁵ T.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A straight wire 0.5 m long carries 4 A and is placed in a uniform field of 0.2 T making an angle of 30° with the wire. The force on the wire is:", options: ["0.4 N", "0.2 N", "0.1 N", "0.8 N"], answer: 1, explain: "F = BIl sinθ = 0.2 × 4 × 0.5 × sin30° = 0.2 N.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A proton and an α-particle enter a uniform magnetic field perpendicularly with the same speed. The ratio of the radii of their circular paths r_p : r_α is:", options: ["1 : 1", "2 : 1", "1 : 4", "1 : 2"], answer: 3, explain: "r = mv/qB. For α: m = 4m_p, q = 2e, so r_α/r_p = (4/2) = 2. Hence r_p : r_α = 1 : 2.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): A charged particle moving in a uniform magnetic field keeps a constant kinetic energy.\nReason (R): The magnetic force is always perpendicular to the velocity and hence does no work on the particle.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "F ⊥ v means W = F·v dt = 0, so the speed (and kinetic energy) does not change; only the direction does.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: A relay uses a solenoid of length 50 cm wound with 500 turns. A current of 4 A passes through it (μ₀ = 4π × 10⁻⁷ T m A⁻¹).\nThe magnetic field inside the solenoid is about:", options: ["2.5 × 10⁻³ T", "5.0 × 10⁻³ T", "5.0 × 10⁻² T", "1.6 × 10⁻³ T"], answer: 1, explain: "n = 500/0.5 = 1000 turns m⁻¹. B = μ₀nI = 4π × 10⁻⁷ × 1000 × 4 = 5.03 × 10⁻³ T.", level: 2 },

      { q: "A galvanometer of resistance 50 Ω gives full-scale deflection for 2 mA. To read currents up to 1 A, the shunt required is nearly:", options: ["1.0 Ω", "0.01 Ω", "0.1 Ω", "5.0 Ω"], answer: 2, explain: "S = I_gG/(I − I_g) = (0.002 × 50)/(1 − 0.002) = 0.1/0.998 ≈ 0.1 Ω.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Case: A galvanometer coil has a resistance of 100 Ω and shows full-scale deflection for 1 mA. A student wants to convert it into a voltmeter of range 0–10 V.\nThe series resistance needed is:", options: ["10000 Ω", "9900 Ω", "100 Ω", "990 Ω"], answer: 1, explain: "Total resistance = V/I_g = 10/0.001 = 10000 Ω. Series resistance = 10000 − 100 = 9900 Ω.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Two long parallel wires 10 cm apart carry 5 A each in the same direction. The force per unit length between them is:", options: ["5 × 10⁻⁵ N/m, repulsive", "1 × 10⁻⁴ N/m, attractive", "2.5 × 10⁻⁵ N/m, attractive", "5 × 10⁻⁵ N/m, attractive"], answer: 3, explain: "f = μ₀I₁I₂/2πd = 2 × 10⁻⁷ × 25 / 0.1 = 5 × 10⁻⁵ N m⁻¹. Parallel currents attract.", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): A current-carrying solenoid behaves like a bar magnet.\nReason (R): The magnetic field outside a solenoid is uniform and equal in magnitude to the field inside it.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 2, explain: "A is true: one end acts as a north pole and the other as a south pole, and the outside field resembles a bar magnet's. R is false: outside the field is weak and non-uniform, while it is uniform inside.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "State the Biot–Savart law in vector form.", a: "The magnetic field due to a current element I dl at a point with position vector r from the element is dB = (μ₀/4π) · I (dl × r) / r³, i.e. magnitude (μ₀/4π) I dl sinθ / r², perpendicular to both dl and r.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Write the expression for the Lorentz force on a charge q moving with velocity v in electric field E and magnetic field B.", a: "F = q(E + v × B)", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Define the ampere using the force between two parallel current-carrying conductors.", a: "Force per unit length between parallel wires is μ₀I₁I₂/2πd. One ampere is that steady current which, flowing in each of two infinitely long, parallel, thin wires placed 1 m apart in vacuum, produces a force of 2 × 10⁻⁷ N per metre of length on each.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Find the magnetic field at a distance of 10 cm from a long straight wire carrying a current of 5 A. State the direction rule.", a: "B = μ₀I/2πr = (4π × 10⁻⁷ × 5)/(2π × 0.1) = 2 × 10⁻⁷ × 5/0.1 = **1 × 10⁻⁵ T**.\nDirection: right-hand thumb rule; the field lines are concentric circles around the wire in the plane perpendicular to it.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "A proton (m = 1.67 × 10⁻²⁷ kg) is accelerated from rest through a potential difference of 1 kV and then enters a uniform magnetic field of 0.1 T perpendicular to its velocity. Find the radius of its path.", a: "Speed: ½mv² = qV → v = √(2qV/m) = √(2 × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 1000 / 1.67 × 10⁻²⁷) ≈ 4.4 × 10⁵ m s⁻¹.\nRadius: r = mv/qB = (1.67 × 10⁻²⁷ × 4.4 × 10⁵)/(1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 0.1) ≈ **4.6 × 10⁻² m (4.6 cm)**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "A galvanometer of resistance 100 Ω shows full-scale deflection at 1 mA. How can it be converted into an ammeter of range 0–100 mA? Find the resistance of the resulting ammeter.", a: "Connect a low resistance (shunt) S in parallel with the galvanometer.\nPotential across both is equal: I_gG = (I − I_g)S → S = (1 × 10⁻³ × 100)/(0.1 − 0.001) = 0.1/0.099 ≈ **1.01 Ω**.\nAmmeter resistance = GS/(G + S) ≈ **1.0 Ω** (very small, so it hardly changes the current in the circuit).", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Using Ampère's circuital law, derive the expression for the magnetic field inside a long solenoid.", a: "1. Consider a long solenoid with n turns per unit length carrying current I; take a rectangular Amperian loop abcd of length h with side ab inside, parallel to the axis.\n2. Field outside is negligible; along bc and da (perpendicular to the axis) B·dl = 0; along cd (outside) B ≈ 0. So ∮B·dl = Bh.\n3. Current enclosed = (nh)I. Ampère's law: Bh = μ₀nhI → **B = μ₀nI**, uniform inside and along the axis.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Using the Biot–Savart law, derive the magnetic field at a point on the axis of a circular current loop of radius R and current I. Hence find the field at the centre.", a: "1. Take an element dl at the top of the loop; P is on the axis at distance x from the centre, so r² = x² + R². The element is perpendicular to r, so dB = (μ₀/4π) I dl / (x² + R²).\n2. dB is perpendicular to r. Its component along the axis is dB sinα, where sinα = R/√(x² + R²); the components perpendicular to the axis cancel for diametrically opposite elements.\n3. Axial component: dB_x = dB sinα = (μ₀I dl/4π) · R / (x² + R²)^(3/2).\n4. Integrate around the loop (∫dl = 2πR): B = μ₀IR² / [2(x² + R²)^(3/2)].\n5. At the centre (x = 0): **B = μ₀I/2R**; for N turns multiply by N. For x ≫ R, B ≈ μ₀IR²/2x³ = μ₀m/2πx³ with m = IπR², like a dipole.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Derive an expression for the torque on a rectangular current-carrying loop placed in a uniform magnetic field. Define its magnetic dipole moment.", a: "1. Loop of sides a (length) and b, N turns, current I, area A = ab, in a field B; normal to the loop makes angle θ with B.\n2. The forces on the two sides of length b are equal, opposite and collinear (along the axis of rotation), so they cancel and give no torque.\n3. The forces on the two sides of length a (perpendicular to B) are each F = NIaB, equal and opposite but not collinear; the perpendicular distance between them is b sinθ.\n4. Torque τ = F × b sinθ = NIaB · b sinθ = **NIAB sinθ**.\n5. Defining magnetic moment m = NIA (direction by the right-hand rule along the normal), τ = m × B. τ is maximum (NIAB) when the plane is parallel to B (θ = 90°) and zero when the plane is perpendicular to B.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: A proton moving at 5 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹ enters a region of uniform magnetic field 0.2 T at right angles to the field. The principle of the cyclotron uses such circular motion. (m_p = 1.67 × 10⁻²⁷ kg)\n(i) Find the radius of the path.\n(ii) Find the time period of revolution.\n(iii) What happens to the period if the proton's speed is doubled (non-relativistic)?", a: "(i) r = mv/qB = (1.67 × 10⁻²⁷ × 5 × 10⁶)/(1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 0.2) = 8.35 × 10⁻²¹ / 3.2 × 10⁻²⁰ ≈ **0.26 m**.\n(ii) T = 2πm/qB = 2π × 1.67 × 10⁻²⁷ / (1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 0.2) ≈ **3.3 × 10⁻⁷ s** (frequency ≈ 3 × 10⁶ Hz).\n(iii) T = 2πm/qB does not depend on speed, so the period stays the same; the radius doubles. This independence of speed is what allows a fixed-frequency alternating voltage to accelerate the particles in a cyclotron.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "magnetism-and-matter": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "According to Gauss's law for magnetism, the net magnetic flux through any closed surface is:", options: ["μ₀ times the pole strength enclosed", "zero", "infinite", "proportional to the number of poles inside"], answer: 1, explain: "Magnetic monopoles do not exist, so field lines are closed loops: whatever enters a closed surface also leaves it. Net flux = 0.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Substances that are weakly repelled by a strong magnet are called:", options: ["paramagnetic", "ferromagnetic", "ionic", "diamagnetic"], answer: 3, explain: "Diamagnetic materials (χ small and negative, e.g. bismuth, copper, water) are weakly repelled by a magnet.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The angle between the magnetic meridian and the geographic meridian at a place is called:", options: ["dip", "declination", "latitude", "inclination"], answer: 1, explain: "Magnetic declination is the angle between the two meridians; the angle of dip is the angle the total field makes with the horizontal.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The SI unit of magnetic dipole moment is:", options: ["A m²", "A m⁻¹", "T m", "Wb"], answer: 0, explain: "m = IA gives ampere × metre² (equivalently J T⁻¹).", level: 1 },
      { q: "On heating a ferromagnetic substance above its Curie temperature, it:", options: ["becomes diamagnetic", "becomes a permanent magnet", "becomes paramagnetic", "becomes a superconductor"], answer: 2, explain: "Above the Curie temperature thermal agitation destroys the domain alignment and the material behaves as a paramagnet.", level: 1 },

      { q: "A bar magnet of magnetic moment 0.5 A m² is placed in a uniform field of 0.4 T at 30° to the field. The torque on it is:", options: ["0.2 N m", "0.05 N m", "0.1 N m", "0.17 N m"], answer: 2, explain: "τ = mB sinθ = 0.5 × 0.4 × sin30° = 0.1 N m.", level: 2 },
      { q: "The magnetic susceptibility of a material is 499. Its relative permeability is:", options: ["499", "501", "1/500", "500"], answer: 3, explain: "μᵣ = 1 + χ = 1 + 499 = 500.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A bar magnet of magnetic moment M is cut into two equal halves by a plane perpendicular to its length. The magnetic moment of each piece is:", options: ["M/2", "M", "2M", "M/4"], answer: 0, explain: "m = (pole strength) × (length). The pole strength is unchanged and the length halves, so each piece has M/2.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): The magnetic susceptibility of a paramagnetic substance decreases as its temperature rises.\nReason (R): Thermal agitation disturbs the alignment of the atomic magnetic dipoles with the applied field.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "Curie's law, χ ∝ 1/T, follows because greater random thermal motion opposes the alignment of dipoles, so the induced magnetisation falls.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: A hiker's compass is used at a place where the horizontal component of Earth's field is 3 × 10⁻⁵ T and the angle of dip is 60°.\nThe total magnetic field of the Earth there is:", options: ["3 × 10⁻⁵ T", "5.2 × 10⁻⁵ T", "1.5 × 10⁻⁵ T", "6 × 10⁻⁵ T"], answer: 3, explain: "B_H = B cosδ → B = B_H/cosδ = 3 × 10⁻⁵ / 0.5 = 6 × 10⁻⁵ T. (The vertical component is B sin60° ≈ 5.2 × 10⁻⁵ T.)", level: 2 },

      { q: "The susceptibility of a paramagnetic sample at 300 K is 2 × 10⁻⁵. At 200 K it will be:", options: ["1.3 × 10⁻⁵", "2 × 10⁻⁵", "3 × 10⁻⁵", "4.5 × 10⁻⁵"], answer: 2, explain: "Curie's law: χ ∝ 1/T. χ₂ = 2 × 10⁻⁵ × (300/200) = 3 × 10⁻⁵.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A bar magnet suspended in a uniform field B oscillates with time period T. If the field is made 4B, the new time period is:", options: ["T/4", "2T", "4T", "T/2"], answer: 3, explain: "T = 2π√(I/mB) ∝ 1/√B. Four times the field halves the period: T/2.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Case: A student places a short bar magnet of magnetic moment 0.4 A m² and measures the field at a point on its axis, 0.2 m from the centre (μ₀/4π = 10⁻⁷ T m A⁻¹).\nThe field at that point is:", options: ["2 × 10⁻⁵ T", "1 × 10⁻⁵ T", "5 × 10⁻⁶ T", "4 × 10⁻⁶ T"], answer: 1, explain: "B_axial = (μ₀/4π)(2m/r³) = 10⁻⁷ × 0.8 / 0.008 = 1 × 10⁻⁵ T. (At an equatorial point at the same distance it would be half, 5 × 10⁻⁶ T.)", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Soft iron is preferred for making the core of an electromagnet.\nReason (R): Soft iron has a large coercivity, so it retains its magnetism after the current is switched off.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 2, explain: "A is true, but the reason is that soft iron has high permeability and LOW coercivity and retentivity, so it magnetises easily and loses magnetism easily. R is false.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "State Gauss's law for magnetism.", a: "The net magnetic flux through any closed surface is zero: ∮B·dS = 0. This shows that isolated magnetic monopoles do not exist and magnetic field lines form closed loops.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Define the angle of dip at a place.", a: "The angle of dip (inclination) is the angle that the Earth's total magnetic field makes with the horizontal in the magnetic meridian at that place.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Distinguish between diamagnetic and paramagnetic substances on the basis of (i) susceptibility and (ii) behaviour in a non-uniform magnetic field.", a: "(i) Diamagnetic: χ is small and negative (μᵣ slightly less than 1). Paramagnetic: χ is small and positive (μᵣ slightly more than 1).\n(ii) In a non-uniform field a diamagnetic substance tends to move from the stronger to the weaker region (repelled), while a paramagnetic substance moves from weaker to stronger field (weakly attracted).", marks: 2 },
      { q: "A bar magnet is cut into two pieces. Explain why we do not get isolated north and south poles.", a: "Each piece again has a north and a south pole, so it is a smaller bar magnet. Magnetic monopoles do not exist; the source of magnetism is current loops/atomic dipoles, which always come as dipoles, and field lines are closed loops (Gauss's law for magnetism).", marks: 2 },
      { q: "A bar magnet of magnetic moment 0.6 A m² is placed in a uniform field of 0.25 T at an angle of 60° to the field. Find (i) the torque on it and (ii) the work done in turning it from the direction of the field to this position.", a: "(i) τ = mB sinθ = 0.6 × 0.25 × sin60° = 0.15 × 0.866 ≈ **0.13 N m**.\n(ii) W = U(60°) − U(0°) = −mB cos60° + mB = mB(1 − cos60°) = 0.15 × 0.5 = **0.075 J**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "What is a hysteresis loop? Define retentivity and coercivity, and state why soft iron is used for electromagnets and steel for permanent magnets.", a: "The B–H (or I–H) curve traced when a ferromagnetic material is taken through a cycle of magnetisation and demagnetisation; B lags behind H.\nRetentivity: the value of B (magnetisation) left when H is reduced to zero. Coercivity: the reverse H needed to bring B to zero.\nSoft iron has low retentivity and coercivity and a thin loop (low energy loss), so it magnetises and demagnetises easily: electromagnets, transformer cores. Steel has high retentivity and high coercivity, so it keeps its magnetism: permanent magnets.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "At a place the horizontal component of the Earth's magnetic field is 3.6 × 10⁻⁵ T and the angle of dip is 60°. Find the vertical component and the total field.", a: "B_H = B cosδ, B_V = B sinδ.\nTotal field B = B_H/cosδ = 3.6 × 10⁻⁵ / 0.5 = **7.2 × 10⁻⁵ T**.\nVertical component B_V = B_H tanδ = 3.6 × 10⁻⁵ × 1.73 ≈ **6.2 × 10⁻⁵ T**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Derive the expression for the potential energy of a magnetic dipole of moment m in a uniform magnetic field B. Discuss the positions of stable and unstable equilibrium.", a: "1. The torque on the dipole at angle θ to the field is τ = mB sinθ.\n2. Work done by an external agent in turning the dipole from θ₁ to θ₂: W = ∫τ dθ = ∫mB sinθ dθ = mB(cosθ₁ − cosθ₂).\n3. This is stored as potential energy U(θ) − U(θ₁). Taking the zero at θ₁ = 90°: **U = −mB cosθ = −m·B**.\n4. Stable equilibrium: θ = 0° (m along B), U = −mB (minimum). Unstable equilibrium: θ = 180°, U = +mB (maximum).\n5. At θ = 90°, U = 0 and the torque is maximum (mB). Left free, the dipole oscillates about the stable position.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Derive the expression for the magnetic field at a point on the axial line of a short bar magnet of moment m, treating it as two poles ±p separated by 2l. Compare it with the equatorial field.", a: "1. Poles: N (+p) and S (−p) at ±l from the centre; magnetic moment m = p · 2l. Point P on the axis at distance r from the centre.\n2. Field due to N: B₁ = (μ₀/4π) p/(r − l)² along the axis away from N; field due to S: B₂ = (μ₀/4π) p/(r + l)² towards S, opposite in direction.\n3. B = (μ₀p/4π)[1/(r − l)² − 1/(r + l)²] = (μ₀p/4π) · 4rl/(r² − l²)².\n4. For r ≫ l: **B_axial = (μ₀/4π) · 2m/r³**, along the direction of m.\n5. At an equatorial point the components along the axis add and the perpendicular ones cancel, giving **B_equatorial = (μ₀/4π) · m/r³**, opposite to m. So B_axial = 2B_equatorial at the same distance.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: Three materials X, Y and Z are tested near a strong electromagnet. The measured magnetic susceptibilities are: X: −2 × 10⁻⁵; Y: +2.3 × 10⁻⁵; Z: +5000.\n(i) Identify the type of magnetic material for X, Y and Z.\n(ii) Which of them will become paramagnetic when heated above a certain temperature, and what is this temperature called?\n(iii) Find the relative permeability of Y and of Z.", a: "(i) X: diamagnetic (χ negative and small). Y: paramagnetic (χ small and positive). Z: ferromagnetic (very large positive χ).\n(ii) Z (the ferromagnetic material); the temperature is the **Curie temperature**, above which the domain structure is lost.\n(iii) μᵣ = 1 + χ. For Y: μᵣ = 1.000023 ≈ **1**. For Z: μᵣ = 1 + 5000 = **5001**.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "electromagnetic-induction": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The SI unit of magnetic flux is:", options: ["tesla", "henry", "weber", "volt"], answer: 2, explain: "Φ = B·A, so 1 weber = 1 T m². (Tesla is the unit of B, henry of inductance.)", level: 1 },
      { q: "Lenz's law is a consequence of conservation of:", options: ["charge", "energy", "momentum", "mass"], answer: 1, explain: "If the induced current aided the change in flux, energy would be created without work being done. Hence the induced current opposes the change.", level: 1 },
      { q: "A conducting rod of length l moves with velocity v perpendicular to a uniform magnetic field B (and to its own length). The motional emf across it is:", options: ["Blv", "Bl/v", "Bv/l", "B²lv"], answer: 0, explain: "The magnetic force on charges in the rod sets up emf = Blv.", level: 1 },
      { q: "One henry is equal to:", options: ["1 V A s", "1 V A⁻¹ s⁻¹", "1 V s⁻¹ A", "1 V s A⁻¹"], answer: 3, explain: "ε = L(dI/dt) gives L = ε/(dI/dt), whose unit is volt second per ampere (also Wb A⁻¹).", level: 1 },
      { q: "Eddy current losses in a transformer core are reduced by:", options: ["using a thick solid iron core", "laminating the core", "increasing the primary current", "using a hollow copper core"], answer: 1, explain: "Thin insulated laminations break up the paths available for eddy currents, reducing the heating loss.", level: 1 },

      { q: "The magnetic flux through a coil of 100 turns changes from 0.01 Wb to 0.05 Wb in 0.2 s. The magnitude of the induced emf is:", options: ["4 V", "2 V", "20 V", "200 V"], answer: 2, explain: "ε = N ΔΦ/Δt = 100 × (0.05 − 0.01)/0.2 = 20 V.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A metal rod 0.5 m long moves at 4 m s⁻¹ perpendicular to a uniform field of 0.3 T, with its length perpendicular to both. The induced emf across its ends is:", options: ["2.4 V", "1.2 V", "0.15 V", "0.6 V"], answer: 3, explain: "ε = Blv = 0.3 × 0.5 × 4 = 0.6 V.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): In a coil rotating in a uniform magnetic field, the induced emf is zero when the plane of the coil is perpendicular to the field.\nReason (R): In that position the flux is maximum, but its rate of change is zero.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "Φ = NBA cosωt is at its maximum when the plane is perpendicular to B, where dΦ/dt = 0. Since ε = −dΦ/dt, the emf is zero there.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: The ignition coil of a petrol engine has an inductance of 0.5 H. A steady current of 2 A flows through it.\nThe energy stored in its magnetic field is:", options: ["0.5 J", "1 J", "2 J", "4 J"], answer: 1, explain: "U = ½LI² = ½ × 0.5 × 2² = 1 J.", level: 2 },
      { q: "When the current in a primary coil changes at a rate of 3 A s⁻¹, an emf of 6 V is induced in a neighbouring secondary coil. The mutual inductance of the pair is:", options: ["0.5 H", "18 H", "2 H", "9 H"], answer: 2, explain: "ε = M (dI/dt) → M = 6/3 = 2 H.", level: 2 },

      { q: "A metal rod of length 0.5 m rotates about one end in a plane perpendicular to a uniform field of 0.4 T with angular speed 20 rad s⁻¹. The emf between its ends is:", options: ["0.5 V", "2 V", "4 V", "1 V"], answer: 3, explain: "ε = ½Bωl² = ½ × 0.4 × 20 × (0.5)² = 1 V.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Case: A student winds 1000 turns of wire on a hollow cardboard tube of length 0.5 m and cross-sectional area 2 × 10⁻⁴ m² to make an air-cored solenoid (μ₀ = 4π × 10⁻⁷ T m A⁻¹).\nIts self-inductance is about:", options: ["0.25 mH", "0.5 mH", "5 mH", "1 mH"], answer: 1, explain: "L = μ₀N²A/l = 4π × 10⁻⁷ × (1000)² × 2 × 10⁻⁴ / 0.5 ≈ 5 × 10⁻⁴ H = 0.5 mH.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Two coils of self-inductances 4 H and 9 H are so wound that all the flux of each links the other (perfect coupling). Their mutual inductance is:", options: ["13 H", "36 H", "2.5 H", "6 H"], answer: 3, explain: "For perfect coupling M = √(L₁L₂) = √(4 × 9) = 6 H.", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): A rectangular loop moving with constant velocity entirely inside a uniform magnetic field has no induced current.\nReason (R): The flux through the loop changes uniformly with time as it moves.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 2, explain: "In a uniform field the flux BA through the loop stays constant while it moves wholly inside the field, so ε = 0 (A is true). The flux does not change, so R is false.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "State Lenz's law.", a: "The polarity of the induced emf is such that it tends to produce a current which opposes the change in magnetic flux that produced it.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Define self-inductance and give its SI unit.", a: "Self-inductance L is the ratio of the flux linkage to the current in a coil (NΦ = LI); equivalently, the emf induced per unit rate of change of current: ε = −L dI/dt. SI unit: henry (H).", marks: 1 },
      { q: "A circular loop of radius 10 cm is placed with its plane perpendicular to a uniform magnetic field of 0.5 T. The field is reduced uniformly to zero in 0.1 s. Find the emf induced in the loop.", a: "A = πr² = π(0.1)² = 0.0314 m². Initial flux = BA = 0.5 × 0.0314 = 0.0157 Wb; final flux = 0.\nε = ΔΦ/Δt = 0.0157/0.1 ≈ **0.16 V**.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "State two applications of eddy currents and one method of reducing them in a transformer core.", a: "Applications (any two): induction furnace for melting metals; electromagnetic braking in trains; magnetic damping in galvanometers/balances; induction cooktops.\nReduction: use a laminated core (thin insulated sheets), which breaks the paths of eddy currents.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "A rod of length 20 cm slides at 5 m s⁻¹ on a U-shaped conducting rail in a uniform magnetic field of 0.4 T perpendicular to the plane. The total resistance of the circuit is 0.4 Ω. Find (i) the induced emf, (ii) the current, (iii) the force needed to keep the rod moving uniformly.", a: "(i) ε = Blv = 0.4 × 0.2 × 5 = **0.4 V**.\n(ii) I = ε/R = 0.4/0.4 = **1 A**.\n(iii) The magnetic force on the rod is F = BIl = 0.4 × 1 × 0.2 = **0.08 N**, opposing the motion. An equal external force is needed. (Check: power Fv = 0.4 W = I²R.)", marks: 3 },
      { q: "The current in a coil of self-inductance 2 H falls uniformly from 3 A to 1 A in 0.1 s. Find the average induced emf and the change in the energy stored in the coil.", a: "ε = L |ΔI/Δt| = 2 × (2/0.1) = **40 V**.\nEnergy change = ½L(I₁² − I₂²) = ½ × 2 × (9 − 1) = **8 J** (released by the coil as the current falls).", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Derive the expression for the motional emf induced in a straight conductor of length l moving with velocity v perpendicular to a uniform magnetic field B.", a: "1. Consider a rod PQ of length l moving at v in a field B perpendicular to its plane (rectangular loop PQRS, with area changing).\n2. Flux through the loop: Φ = Blx, where x is the length of the loop (changing with time).\n3. ε = −dΦ/dt = −Bl(dx/dt) = **Blv** in magnitude. (Equivalently, the Lorentz force qvB on free charges produces a field and emf of Blv.)", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Derive the expression for the self-inductance of a long air-cored solenoid and the energy stored in an inductor.", a: "1. Long solenoid, N turns, length l, area A, n = N/l. Current I gives B = μ₀nI.\n2. Flux through each turn Φ = BA = μ₀nIA; flux linkage NΦ = μ₀nNAI = μ₀n²AlI.\n3. Since NΦ = LI: **L = μ₀n²Al = μ₀N²A/l** (multiplied by μᵣ with a core).\nEnergy: 4. While the current is changing, the back emf is L dI/dt; the power supplied against it is P = εI = LI dI/dt.\n5. Total work to raise the current from 0 to I: W = ∫₀^I LI dI = **½LI²**, stored in the magnetic field (energy density B²/2μ₀).", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Derive the mutual inductance of two long coaxial solenoids of the same length l, with the inner solenoid S₁ (radius r₁, N₁ turns) lying completely inside the outer solenoid S₂ (radius r₂, N₂ turns).", a: "1. Pass a current I₁ through S₁. Field inside it: B₁ = μ₀(N₁/l)I₁.\n2. Flux linked with one turn of S₂ is only through the area of S₁: Φ₂ = B₁ · πr₁² = μ₀N₁πr₁²I₁/l.\n3. Flux linkage with S₂: N₂Φ₂ = μ₀N₁N₂πr₁²I₁/l.\n4. By definition N₂Φ₂ = M₂₁I₁, hence **M = μ₀N₁N₂πr₁²/l**.\n5. The same expression is obtained by passing a current through S₂ (M₁₂ = M₂₁); M depends on geometry and medium only, and the unit is the henry. Induced emf in S₂ is ε₂ = −M dI₁/dt.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: A square loop of side 10 cm and resistance 2 Ω lies in a uniform magnetic field of 0.5 T directed into the page, with the loop's plane perpendicular to the field. It is pulled out of the field region at uniform speed in 0.2 s.\n(i) Find the average emf induced.\n(ii) Find the current and state its direction.\n(iii) Find the heat produced in the loop.", a: "(i) Initial flux = BA = 0.5 × (0.1)² = 5 × 10⁻³ Wb; final flux 0. ε = 5 × 10⁻³ / 0.2 = **0.025 V**.\n(ii) I = ε/R = 0.025/2 = **0.0125 A (12.5 mA)**. The inward flux decreases, so by Lenz's law the induced current opposes this by producing an inward field: **clockwise** as seen from the front.\n(iii) H = ε²t/R = (0.025)² × 0.2/2 = **6.25 × 10⁻⁵ J**.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "alternating-current": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The rms value of an alternating current of peak value I₀ is:", options: ["I₀/2", "I₀√2", "I₀/√2", "2I₀/π"], answer: 2, explain: "I_rms = I₀/√2 ≈ 0.707 I₀.", level: 1 },
      { q: "In an ideal inductor connected to an AC source, the current:", options: ["leads the voltage by 90°", "is in phase with the voltage", "lags the voltage by 45°", "lags the voltage by 90°"], answer: 3, explain: "For a pure inductor the voltage leads the current by π/2, i.e. the current lags the voltage by 90°.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The resonant frequency of a series LCR circuit is:", options: ["1/(2π√(LC))", "2π√(LC)", "1/(2πLC)", "√(L/C)/2π"], answer: 0, explain: "Resonance occurs when X_L = X_C, i.e. ω₀L = 1/ω₀C, so f₀ = 1/(2π√(LC)).", level: 1 },
      { q: "A transformer works on the principle of:", options: ["self-induction", "mutual induction", "the Hall effect", "the heating effect of current"], answer: 1, explain: "A changing current in the primary changes the flux linked with the secondary, inducing an emf there (mutual induction).", level: 1 },
      { q: "The power factor of a purely resistive AC circuit is:", options: ["0", "0.5", "1", "∞"], answer: 2, explain: "Voltage and current are in phase (φ = 0), so cosφ = 1.", level: 1 },

      { q: "The household supply is quoted as 220 V (rms). Its peak value is nearly:", options: ["311 V", "220 V", "155 V", "440 V"], answer: 0, explain: "V₀ = √2 × V_rms = 1.414 × 220 ≈ 311 V.", level: 2 },
      { q: "An inductor of 0.1 H is connected to a 50 Hz supply. Its reactance is nearly:", options: ["3.14 Ω", "31.4 Ω", "314 Ω", "0.5 Ω"], answer: 1, explain: "X_L = 2πfL = 2π × 50 × 0.1 = 31.4 Ω.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): A capacitor blocks direct current but allows alternating current to pass.\nReason (R): The capacitive reactance X_C = 1/(ωC) is infinite for DC (ω = 0) and finite for AC.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "For DC the capacitor charges up and then no current flows, equivalent to infinite reactance. For AC, X_C is finite, so the capacitor charges and discharges every cycle.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: A mobile phone charger steps down the 220 V mains to 5.5 V using a transformer whose primary has 4000 turns.\nThe number of turns on the secondary coil is:", options: ["50", "160", "200", "100"], answer: 3, explain: "Ns/Np = Vs/Vp → Ns = 4000 × 5.5/220 = 100 turns.", level: 2 },
      { q: "In a series LCR circuit R = 30 Ω, X_L = 60 Ω and X_C = 20 Ω. The impedance is:", options: ["110 Ω", "70 Ω", "50 Ω", "10 Ω"], answer: 2, explain: "Z = √(R² + (X_L − X_C)²) = √(900 + 1600) = 50 Ω.", level: 2 },

      { q: "Case: In a radio, a series circuit with L = 0.1 H, C = 10 μF and R = 50 Ω is used for tuning.\nThe quality factor of the circuit at resonance is:", options: ["0.5", "2", "20", "200"], answer: 1, explain: "ω₀ = 1/√(LC) = 1/√(10⁻⁶) = 1000 rad s⁻¹. Q = ω₀L/R = 1000 × 0.1/50 = 2.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A coil with R = 30 Ω and X_L = 40 Ω is connected to a 100 V (rms) AC source. The average power consumed is:", options: ["200 W", "160 W", "80 W", "120 W"], answer: 3, explain: "Z = √(30² + 40²) = 50 Ω, I = 100/50 = 2 A. P = I²R = 4 × 30 = 120 W (power factor R/Z = 0.6).", level: 3 },
      { q: "Case: A series LCR circuit is connected to a 220 V (rms) source with R = 44 Ω, and is adjusted to resonance where X_L = X_C = 100 Ω.\nThe rms voltage across the inductor is:", options: ["220 V", "500 V", "44 V", "100 V"], answer: 1, explain: "At resonance Z = R, so I = 220/44 = 5 A. V_L = I X_L = 5 × 100 = 500 V, larger than the source voltage.", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): The average value of a sinusoidal alternating current over a complete cycle is zero.\nReason (R): The rms value of a sinusoidal alternating current over a complete cycle is also zero.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 2, explain: "Positive and negative half-cycles cancel, so the mean is zero (A true). The rms value is I₀/√2, not zero, because it averages i² which is always positive (R false).", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Why can a transformer not be used to step up or step down a DC voltage?", a: "A transformer needs a changing flux in the core. DC produces a constant flux, so no emf is induced in the secondary (apart from a momentary one when switching on or off).", marks: 1 },
      { q: "State the condition for resonance in a series LCR circuit and the value of the power factor at resonance.", a: "Resonance occurs when X_L = X_C, i.e. ω₀ = 1/√(LC). Then Z = R (minimum), the current is maximum, and the power factor = 1.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "A 100 Ω resistor is connected to a 220 V, 50 Hz AC supply. Find the rms current and the peak current.", a: "I_rms = V_rms/R = 220/100 = **2.2 A**.\nI₀ = √2 × I_rms = 1.414 × 2.2 ≈ **3.1 A**.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "An ideal transformer has 100 turns in the primary and 2000 turns in the secondary. The primary is connected to 220 V and carries a current of 5 A. Find the secondary voltage and current.", a: "Vs = Vp(Ns/Np) = 220 × 2000/100 = **4400 V**.\nFor an ideal transformer VpIp = VsIs: Is = (220 × 5)/4400 = **0.25 A**.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "A series LCR circuit has R = 30 Ω, L = 0.6 H and C = 500 μF and is connected to a 100 V (rms) supply of angular frequency 100 rad s⁻¹. Find (i) the impedance, (ii) the rms current, (iii) the power factor and the average power.", a: "X_L = ωL = 100 × 0.6 = 60 Ω; X_C = 1/(ωC) = 1/(100 × 5 × 10⁻⁴) = 20 Ω.\n(i) Z = √(R² + (X_L − X_C)²) = √(900 + 1600) = **50 Ω**.\n(ii) I = V/Z = 100/50 = **2 A**.\n(iii) cosφ = R/Z = 30/50 = **0.6** (current lags). P = VI cosφ = 100 × 2 × 0.6 = **120 W** (= I²R).", marks: 3 },
      { q: "A series LCR circuit has L = 5 mH, C = 20 μF and R = 10 Ω, and is connected to a 100 V (rms) variable-frequency source. Find the resonant frequency, the current at resonance and the quality factor.", a: "ω₀ = 1/√(LC) = 1/√(5 × 10⁻³ × 20 × 10⁻⁶) = 1/√(10⁻⁷) ≈ 3162 rad s⁻¹, so f₀ = ω₀/2π ≈ **503 Hz**.\nAt resonance Z = R: I = 100/10 = **10 A**.\nQ = ω₀L/R = 3162 × 5 × 10⁻³ / 10 ≈ **1.6**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Show that the rms value of an alternating current i = i₀ sin ωt is i₀/√2.", a: "1. The rms value is the square root of the mean of i² over one cycle: i_rms² = ⟨i₀² sin²ωt⟩.\n2. sin²ωt = (1 − cos2ωt)/2, and the average of cos2ωt over a cycle is zero, so ⟨sin²ωt⟩ = 1/2.\n3. i_rms² = i₀²/2, hence **i_rms = i₀/√2 ≈ 0.707 i₀**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Using a phasor diagram, derive the expression for the impedance and phase angle of a series LCR circuit connected to an AC source v = v₀ sin ωt. State the condition of resonance.", a: "1. Same current i = i₀ sin(ωt − φ) flows through R, L and C. Voltages: V_R = iR (in phase with i), V_L = iX_L (leads i by 90°), V_C = iX_C (lags i by 90°).\n2. Phasor diagram: V_R along the current, V_L upward, V_C downward; the net reactive part is V_L − V_C.\n3. Source voltage V² = V_R² + (V_L − V_C)² = I²[R² + (X_L − X_C)²].\n4. **Impedance Z = √(R² + (ωL − 1/ωC)²)** and I = V/Z.\n5. **Phase angle tanφ = (X_L − X_C)/R**: current lags if X_L > X_C, leads if X_C > X_L.\nResonance: when X_L = X_C, φ = 0, Z = R (minimum), current is maximum and ω₀ = 1/√(LC).", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Explain the principle, construction and working of a transformer. Derive the relation between the voltages and turns, and state four causes of energy loss.", a: "Principle: mutual induction between two coils on a common soft-iron core.\nConstruction: primary (Np turns) and secondary (Ns turns) wound on a laminated soft-iron core.\nWorking: 1. An alternating voltage Vp across the primary produces a changing flux φ in the core, which links both coils.\n2. Induced emf per turn is the same in both coils: ε_p = −Np dφ/dt, ε_s = −Ns dφ/dt.\n3. For negligible resistance in the primary, Vp ≈ ε_p and Vs = ε_s, so **Vs/Vp = Ns/Np = k**.\n4. For an ideal transformer VpIp = VsIs, so Is/Ip = Np/Ns. Step-up: Ns > Np (voltage rises, current falls); step-down: Ns < Np.\nLosses: (a) flux leakage; (b) resistance of the windings (Joule heating); (c) eddy currents in the core (reduced by lamination); (d) hysteresis loss (reduced by using soft iron); also humming due to magnetostriction.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: A power station delivers 220 kW to a distant town through transmission lines of total resistance 5 Ω. The power is first transmitted at 2200 V and, in another trial, at 22000 V.\n(i) Find the current in the lines and the power lost at 2200 V.\n(ii) Find the current and the power lost at 22000 V.\n(iii) Why is power transmitted at high voltage, and what is the role of transformers?", a: "(i) I = P/V = 220000/2200 = **100 A**. Loss = I²R = 100² × 5 = **50 kW** (almost 23% of the power).\n(ii) I = 220000/22000 = **10 A**. Loss = 10² × 5 = **500 W** (about 0.2%).\n(iii) For a given power, a higher voltage means a smaller current, and since loss = I²R the loss falls as 1/V². Step-up transformers at the station raise the voltage for transmission; step-down transformers near the town lower it to a safe 220 V for use.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  }
});
